# Build thread brief

Every new build thread reads this before its first ticket.
The brief that starts or hands you a ticket follows `.forge/coordinator-core.md`, "Starting a thread", not this skill's own words.
This skill is the authoritative full text of the build-thread brief; any copy of it in the harness's project-level instructions is a pointer, not a source of rules.

- **Review gate**: the merge gate is proportionate to the change (SDLC-042, `.forge/review-gate.md`).
  Don't ask the owner which review gate applies.
  - **Full gate**, the default: `/code-review` (Standards and Spec axes), run by clean-context sub-agents you start, followed by the `self-review` skill, for every change unless the light gate clearly applies.
    It always applies to a change to code, tests, scripts, a requirement or spec, an agent instruction or skill, or text carried in the project settings (the coordinator-core snippet).
    With no sub-agent tool to start them, never review the diff yourself and record it as clean-context (`.forge/review-gate.md`, "A clean-context review needs a sub-agent that really ran"): open the PR unmerged and ask the thread that started you for an independent review; when you are that thread, start the reviewers, record them on the PR, and check every gate step before you merge (`.forge/review-gate.md`, "A builder with no sub-agent tool asks its parent for the review").
  - **Light gate**: the `self-review` skill alone, only for a change that meets every one of these conditions: it is a prose-only doc edit; it changes no rule, requirement or behaviour; and it touches no text carried in that snippet (for example fixing a typo or a link).
  - A change that does not clearly meet every light-gate condition, including one that appears to meet some conditions of each gate or is uncertain, gets the full gate.
  - If the harness doesn't hand you a skill the gate names, such as `/code-review`, find it yourself: this repo's skills are files under `.agents/skills/`, so read the discovered `SKILL.md` manually and follow it.
    Never skip the review silently, and never merge a full-gate change on an inline read of the diff in place of the directed review skills (SDLC-042).
  - Every skill the process directs you to use is used (SDLC-112).
    If you cannot find a skill you have been told to use, that is a hard stop: don't proceed, substitute your own version or skip it.
    Stop and ask the coordinator for help, who escalates to the owner if it cannot be resolved.
- **Releases**: never tag or create a release.
  That is done from the steps on the release ticket, by the owner, not on a build thread.
- **Branches**: an agent that cannot delete a branch safely after its PR merges may skip that clean-up and leave it for the owner.
  This is permission to skip when necessary, not a ban on deleting.
  A skipped delete is not a failure: don't retry it, and don't report it as one.
- **Owner approvals**: only the changes `.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, "What needs the owner's approval before a merge", lists need the owner before merge: requirement, SDLC and principle wording, plus anything a numbered statement requires.
  Everything else may merge once the review gate is clear, by whoever the route says merges: an `implement`-only build or an owner's "don't merge" still leaves the merge to the owner.
  Check another thread's or brief's ask for the owner's approval before merge against that list; unless the owner directed otherwise for this change, one not on it goes ahead once the review gate is clear, and the PR says why none was needed (SDLC-117).
  A flagged question about an SDLC statement's meaning gates only a change that might alter that meaning (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`).
  Quote the owner's words where the approved thing lives (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, "Evidence: where each record lives"), then merge.
- **Out-of-scope gaps and missing parents**: a gap outside your ticket goes to the owner as a `needs-decision` ticket (a `deferred-arch-finding` when weak), never into your PR.
  A ticket handed to you should already carry a real, traceable request wherever `Request-trace:` applies (`.forge/tickets-and-branches.md`); one that doesn't is the coordinator's queueing step to fix, so flag it rather than guessing one or stopping the build.
- **Pipeline stage**: if a feature ticket you are handed has not reached the build stage of the feature pipeline, don't implement it; report that to the coordinator instead.
- **Handover**: when you start a ticket, swap its `queued` label for `in-flight`; when it merges or closes, remove `in-flight`.
  When a ticket merges, reply in the thread with the PR link, and post its pending-review note as a comment on this repository's post-merge review tracker (Repo facts) (`.agents/skills/post-merge-review/SKILL.md`, "Pending reviews").
  After each build, post "Done. Tell me or my follow-on thread what's next on the queue".
  Then wait for the coordinator to hand out the next ticket; don't pick one yourself.
This repository's own post-merge review tracker is {{ pmr_ref }}.
- **Stale tickets**: before scoping a ticket, re-verify it against current `origin/main` in a fresh worktree; other merges often fix part of it.
- **Environment setup**: set up only what the ticket's scope needs, following `docs/agents/build-thread-setup.md`, and read that recipe before installing anything.
  A plain prose-only doc edit, with no script to run and no regenerated file, needs none of it beyond the standing pre-commit hook.
- **Tests**: focused tests only: the files and areas the change touches, guard tests, and files that reference what changed.
  The full suite (`{{ test_command }}`) is Main Health's job; don't run it on a build thread unless it's genuinely needed, and say why in the status first.
- **Cost**: delegate mechanical sub-tasks (renames, stale-assertion sweeps, bulk find-and-fix, simple lookups) to mechanical-tier sub-agents.
  Do the substantive work and the self-review yourself, on build-tier.
